WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.920
Spacex Crew Dragon Grace reached the International Space Station in seven hours and 55 minutes, the fastest US spacecraft transit on record. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 214. Crew Dragon Grace docked at the Harmony Module Forward port seven hours and 55 minutes after liftoff from Cape Canaveral. NASA confirmed the flight as the fastest launch to docking of any US spacecraft in International Space Station history.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:35.460
The previous US record was held by an earlier Dragon cargo mission that took just over 12 hours.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.940
The rapid transit resulted from favorable orbital phasing rather than new hardware.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.720
The crew included NASA astronauts Jessica Watkins and Luke Delaney,

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.320
Canadian Space Agency astronaut Joshua Kutrick,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.500
and Ross Cosmos Cosmonaut Sirgate Teteriatnikov.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.740
While commercial crew operations keep accelerating,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.500
Government agencies are also locking in long-term plans beyond the ISS.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:08.440
The European Space Agency signed a memorandum of understanding with vast at the 77th International

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.820
Astronautical Congress.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:16.480
The agreement builds on a 2024 MOU and four already planned crewed missions to the International

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.680
Space Station and Haven 1.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.920
It creates a framework for potential European astronaut flights aboard Haven Station.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.960
ESA hardware contributions, jointly defined research campaigns, and dedicated science accommodations

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:38.120
after the International Space Station ends operations. ESA Director General Joseph Ashbocker noted

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.580
that the International Space Station has been one of the greatest examples of international cooperation

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:50.180
in space. Director of Human and Robotic Exploration Daniel Nguyen-Schwander said the cooperation

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.260
Operation helps Europe prepare for the post-ISS era.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:59.740
VAST's CEO Max Howell said the company is designing Haven Station to ensure continued

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.000
European access to low-earth orbit.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.420
The Sharjah Space and Astronomy Hub ran a week-long program of lectures, planetarium shows,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.440
and interactive stations during World Space Week.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:17.560
Events included a talk on the hidden power of space exploration, a history of rockets traced

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.740
through physical models and stations where visitors designed mission patches.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:28.700
Director Professor Hamid M. K. Al-Naymi said the hub uses interactive methods to nurture

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:31.640
scientific curiosity among younger generations.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:38.420
Acting Planetarium Director Fatima Muhammad Al-Katri noted that the activities combined knowledge

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:41.140
with experience to deepen passion for space.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.060
The program aligns with the United Nations established World Space Week theme.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:52.100
Journalists can register by October 30th to attend the European Space Agency's Intermediate

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:57.720
Ministerial Meeting in Rome on December 15th, where member states will discuss Europe's

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.300
future in space exploration.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:06.300
As agencies plan for the post-ISS era, researchers are still finding brand new worlds forming

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:06.920
right now.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:14.460
A team from Diego Portales University identified Elias 2-24b as the youngest confirmed

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:20.080
exoplanet to date. Researchers re-analyzed coronagraph observations from the European

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:27.880
Southern Observatories' very large telescope and compared 2018 and 2020 data from the WM

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:34.180
Keck Observatory in Hawaii. The planet appears as a gravitationally bound point-of-light inside a

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:40.520
gap in the disk around the star Elias 2-24. The gap is thought to have been carved by the forming

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:45.840
planet. Traditional transit methods miss such young worlds because they are still embedded

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.600
in dense disks of gas and dust. While newborn planets are being spotted in distant disks,

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:58.260
closer to home, a joint ESA cast mission has captured something dramatic in our own atmosphere.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:05.200
The ESA cast's smile mission recorded a full auroral oval visible even during daylight hours.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:11.200
The time-lapse video covers roughly one hour in late July and shows the oval's turbulent

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.300
variability in ultraviolet light.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.560
Stars appear to drift across the frame because the spacecraft's camera view shifts during

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:19.100
its orbit.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.480
One goal of smile is to study how the solar wind interacts with Earth's magnetosphere to

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.240
improve protection of astronauts, spacecraft, and power grids.

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:35.760
The operational land imager on Landsat 9 captured rivers carrying colored dissolved organic

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.480
matter into Hanna and Rupert Bay's on September 27, 2026.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:46.260
The brown staining comes from tannins and lignins leached from decaying vegetation in the

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.960
boreal forests of northern Quebec and Ontario.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:55.340
Postglacial rebound has created shallow, sloping mud flats where river sediment mixes with

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.640
incoming tidal water.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.000
Green dandritic patterns on shore reflect differences in moisture and vegetation.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.700
Engineers at NASA are also enhancing test facilities to study icing caused by

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:13.140
unusually large, very cold water droplets in clouds, a hazard that affects

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.480
aircraft performance. Understanding space weather around Earth also helps explain

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:24.340
extreme environments near black holes. Our next story takes us there. After six

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:30.280
months at Lacta Charms, beyond Jezero Crater's Western Rim, Perseverance encountered a field

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:31.660
of light-toned boulders.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:37.120
Data indicate many are gabro formed by slow cooling of magma deep in the crust.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:43.120
The rover is investigating a possible bedrock outcrop of angular rock fragments that may be

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:44.800
the source of the scattered boulders.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:50.540
The team is using arm-mounted instruments at the Idubi site to analyze composition and

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:50.940
texture.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:57.320
New analysis of Hubble observation suggests a planet candidate is accruing onto a white dwarf,

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:03.880
implying a second-generation planetary system. The work revives an earlier cold case by showing

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:10.080
material from a disrupted body falling onto the stellar remnant. Such systems would form after the

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.860
star's red giant phase destroyed any original planets. The study presents evidence that a planet

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:21.080
planet formed after the host star became a white dwarf is now losing material to the star

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.280
through spectroscopic signatures of ongoing accretion.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.420
This would be the first known example of a planet forming in the debris disk of a stellar

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.040
remnant.

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:35.620
The report summarizes the Hubble and nature findings on a planet candidate orbiting a white

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:40.800
dwarf and notes that the system would represent the first detected case of planet formation

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.220
after a star's death.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:48.280
From Mars's surface geology, we shift to the most extreme gravitational environment in the galaxy.

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:55.180
Researchers modeled how a neutron inside the Ergosphere of Sagittarius A-Star could decay into

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.780
a proton, electron, and neutrino while extracting rotational energy.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:05.660
The Ergosphere is the region where frame dragging is so strong that nothing can remain stationary.

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:12.920
The process, first proposed by Roger Penrose in 1969, allows a particle to escape with

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:16.180
more energy than it entered by leaving negative energy behind.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.400
Magnetic field interactions could make the mechanism far more efficient than the basic

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:21.860
version.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:25.460
The study appears on the R. Archive Preprint Server.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.340
We close today with a quick look at how orbital life affects the people living it.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.820
RCSI University's Dr. Ronan Lorden reviewed how a 90-minute orbital period creates 16 sunrises

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:42.780
and sunsets per day, breaking the usual light-dark cues for the circadian clock.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.120
Two biological systems normally align sleep pressure and circadian timing, but spaceflight

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.060
can separate them.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:56.140
Astronauts receive 8.5 hours of scheduled sleep opportunity, yet the mismatch can leave

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:59.020
them tired without being biologically ready for sleep.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:04.560
Tailored lighting, exercise timing, and meal scheduling are used to mitigate the effects.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:10.280
A University of Central Florida alumnus, now at NASA, is contributing to planning for

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:16.480
the first crude lunar base, with work focused on habitat systems and surface operations infrastructure.

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:22.120
Next time, we'll be watching for updates on how those mitigation strategies perform

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.800
during upcoming crude missions.

